{"name":"Rocket Fuel","hex":"#fefbe5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rocket","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A projectile.\nA cylindrical projectile that can be fired to a great height through combustion,  a type of firework of this form, typically exploding with light and colour; a skyrocket. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}\nA blunt lance head used in jousting.\nA long vehicle or craft propelled by a rocket engine; a missile or rocket-propelled spacecraft. \n\nAn engine operating similarly to the pyrotechnic, generating thrust by the expulsion of hot gases; a rocket engine.","A cylindrical projectile that can be fired to a great height through combustion,  a type of firework of this form, typically exploding with light and colour; a skyrocket. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To accelerate swiftly and powerfully.","To fly vertically."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rocket"},{"term":"fuel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Substance consumed to provide energy through combustion, or through chemical or nuclear reaction.","Substance that provides nourishment for a living organism; food."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To provide with fuel.","To exacerbate, to cause to grow or become greater."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/fuel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rocket propellant","blocks":[{"text":"Rocket propellant is used as a reaction mass ejected from a rocket engine to produce thrust. The energy required can either come from the propellants themselves, as with a chemical rocket, or from an external source, as with ion engines or nuclear engines."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Delta_IV_launch_2013-08-28.jpg/330px-Delta_IV_launch_2013-08-28.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rocket_propellant"},{"title":"Rocket","blocks":[{"text":"A rocket is an elongated flying vehicle that uses a rocket engine to accelerate without using any surrounding air. A rocket engine produces thrust by reaction to exhaust expelled at high speed. Unlike jet engines, rockets are fuelled entirely by propellant which they carry, without the need for oxygen from air; consequently a rocket can fly in the vacuum of space, and indeed rocket engines operate more efficiently outside the atmosphere."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9a/Soyuz_TMA-9_launch.jpg/330px-Soyuz_TMA-9_launch.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rocket"},{"title":"Fuel","blocks":[{"text":"A fuel is any material that can react with other substances to release energy as thermal energy or to be used for work. The concept was originally applied solely to those materials capable of releasing chemical energy but has since also been applied to other sources of heat energy, such as nuclear energy."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Heinrich_Zille_Herbst.jpg/330px-Heinrich_Zille_Herbst.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fuel"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Oldest_depiction_of_rocket_arrows.jpg/330px-Oldest_depiction_of_rocket_arrows.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6f/Oldest_depiction_of_rocket_arrows.jpg/330px-Oldest_depiction_of_rocket_arrows.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rocket arrows depicted in the Huolongjing: \"fire arrow\", \"dragon-shaped arrow frame\", and a \"complete fire arrow\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOldest_depiction_of_rocket_arrows.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/